# Line-ending policy.
#
# Host-side shell scripts must stay LF: a clone on Windows with
# core.autocrlf=true would otherwise give them CRLF, and bash rejects the
# shebang line. Inputs must stay LF because the engine's awk parser treats a
# trailing \r as part of the field value (a 15-residue sequence then fails with
# "ERROR 2 : invalid residue at 16").
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.inp   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.cff   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Media are binary. Git's heuristics would almost certainly get this right on
# their own, but a mangled byte in a video is invisible until someone tries to
# play it, so say so explicitly.
*.mp4   -text -diff
*.webp  -text -diff
*.png   -text -diff
*.gif   -text -diff

# The engine is committed with LF. Its content is otherwise as delivered by its
# author, so git must not renormalise it or diff it as prose — a .lib file is a
# fixed-column table and a whitespace-normalising diff of one is unreadable.
# The container runs dos2unix at build time regardless, which covers a Windows
# checkout made with core.autocrlf=true.
engine/** -text
